Chapter 153.

Side Story: Bird of Paradise (1).





The door to the study remained firmly shut late into the night.
Matthias narrowed his eyes as he saw the light seeping out through the crack under the door. For several nights in a row, the duchess had been staying up until the early morning, studying. This pattern would likely continue until she was done with her final exams.
He knocked gently at the door, and then slowly opened it. Layla was asleep at her desk. Her glasses were still on, which suggested that she had not meant to fall asleep.
He gazed at her for a short while, then slowly approached her. As he got closer, he snickered as he noticed the pen that was still firmly gripped in her tiny hand.
Leaning against the desk, he looked down at his peacefully sleeping wife and wondered if he should wake her.

It was a serene, early-summer night. A breeze wafted in through the open window, infusing the study with the faint smell of roses.
Rather than calling out to her, he decided to caress her golden hair, which was fluttering in the breeze. She soon stirred, and after a moment blinked open her eyes.
“Hi, Layla,” he whispered, stooping down closer to her.
She rubbed her bleary eyes under her glasses for a moment. Once her eyes had regained their focus, she sat up with a start, then hurriedly fixed her glasses and straightened her blouse.
“I was just resting my eyes,” she explained. Matthias just smiled, saying nothing in response.
“I really was…” she muttered, lowering her eyes. She could feel her fingers trembling as she touched her faintly blushing cheeks. She couldn’t believe that she was feeling so flustered in front of the duke, when they had been married for almost a year. She put her disheveled hair into a ponytail before looking at him again.
He was quietly gazing out the window at the night sky. With nothing but the desk lamp illuminating the room, it was dim, but she could still sense his presence vividly. As she reflected on this, she realized that it had always been that way with him. Ever since the summer day all those years ago when she had first encountered him, he had stood out distinctly in her awareness. She hadn’t always liked this, but it had always been true.
Suddenly he turned his head towards her and their eyes met. She flinched, then said, “Matty?”
He nodded in response, while a slightly mischievous grin lingered on his lips.
“Would you mind leaving me alone?” she asked.

“Why? So you can go back to sleep?”
“No!” she replied, shaking her head. Just then, she noticed the large ink stain on her notes. She tried her best to hide her embarrassment over this. “I’m going to keep studying.”
“Oh, are you?”
“Yes!” she insisted, conspicuously flipping through the pages of the textbook to show that she was serious.
She felt his soft lips against her cheek, and then she heard his equally soft laughter resonating through the dim room.
“Go ahead,” he said. “Study.”
“How am I supposed to do that with you here?”
“Don’t mind me. I won’t distract you. I’ll be quiet,” he brazenly replied, his face still close to hers. He tilted his head slightly, and his bangs brushed against her cheek, tickling her.
She tried to stay calm, but she knew it would be impossible to get any studying done with him next to her like this. Even Felix would have been less distracting. Rather than saying this, she just frowned, and tried to ignore the burning feeling in her flushed cheeks. “Get out of here, please. I really have to study.”
“I thought you were the top student in the class. Shouldn’t you have studied for the exam in advance?”
“Obviously I did!” she shouted. As soon as the words were out of her mouth, she realized that he was playing around with her.
He snickered and stood up straight. His shadow enveloped her. “Then why are you cramming at the last minute like this?”
“Well,” she replied, “just in case I missed something.” Despite her efforts to remain calm, the bright-red color on her cheeks was visible even in the

darkness.
Even though it wouldn’t have been the end of the world for her to slip from her spot as the top student in the class, she was determined not to let that happen. She felt that this was especially important because she was a female student, and even more so because she was Duchess Herhardt, the subject of a great deal of gossip. Many of the students in the class were cold to her for one reason or another, and she didn’t want to let them win.
Matthias nodded, seeming to understand how she felt. He gently caressed her cheek, and then straightened her glasses for her.
She felt a sense of relief, along with a strange sense of longing. She cocked her head slightly. No, not right now… She shook her head to try to rid herself of the feelings that were bubbling up inside her, but it was no use. She wished that he would leave, but he remained in the same spot, staring intently at her with a calm, emotionless expression on his face.
She lowered her head to hide her reluctant excitement. Then she heard another chuckle. He’s messing with me again, she thought.
Before she knew it, his lips were touching hers. The sound of her glasses being deftly removed and placed on the desk mingled with the sound of her quiet moaning. After a few quick pecks, it didn’t take long for the kisses to grow deeper and more passionate.
Instead of struggling or pushing him away, she put her arms around his neck. His hair felt much softer than she remembered it. It felt just like the identically colored hair of their young son, Felix.
When the kisses, which had started out as gentle as the summer breeze, finally ended, her cheeks were as brightly colored as the roses in the vase on the desk. As she struggled to catch her breath, she noticed with disbelief how calm and composed he was.

His face neared hers again, and she reflexively closed her eyes in anticipation of another kiss. However, she didn’t feel the warmth of his gentle breathing. Perplexed, she opened her eyes, and felt something being placed on the bridge of her nose. He had put her glasses back on her face for her.
“You’ve got to study, Layla,” he whispered, as he wiped the saliva from her lips with his finger. “Study hard,” he added, before stepping away.
His abrupt withdrawal made her feel even more flustered.
He casually walked away from the desk, before suddenly turning around just as he reached the door. He didn’t seem fazed at all by the frown on her face.
“Will you please depart, Your Grace?” she asked, putting on a coy air of formality. “I’m really going to study now. I’m going to study so hard!” she added, picking up the pen with determination.
In response, he gave her a deep bow, as if he were a subject having an audience with a queen. She glared at him for a moment, irritated that he was teasing her like this. Soon, however, she couldn’t help but laugh.
Having gotten the reaction he wanted, he turned and sauntered out of the room. When she heard the door latch shut, she let out a long sigh.
He’s so weird.
She shook her head to try to clear her thoughts, but she couldn’t rid herself of the strange excitement that his visit had left her with.
What a weird guy he is.
After rubbing her cheeks, which were still warm, she picked up the pen once again. Now I’ve really got to study hard, just like my annoying husband said.







Just as he had done the previous semester, Professor Lorenz posted the students’ final grades on the wall for all to see. This practice of his bothered some of the students, but none of them had the courage to confront him about it.
Layla snaked her way through the crowd, trying to get close enough to find her name on the list. Professor Lorenz was always the last instructor in the biology department to announce the grades for his class. Thus, the grades the students received today would also determine their ranking throughout the whole department.
Before Layla could find her name on the list, she heard a sarcastic voice addressing her, saying, “Congratulations, Duchess. You must be very pleased.”
She turned towards the voice and was not surprised to see Count Lemon. He always vied with her for the position of top student, but generally had to settle for second place. This fact alone would have soured his attitude towards her, but he seemed especially indignant that he was coming second to a woman. And not just any woman, but a woman who had been a lowly mistress before becoming a duchess.
“I must say, Duchess Herhardt, you seem to be able to get whatever you want in life, be it a good marriage, or good grades.”
His derisive remark was met with laughter from various other students nearby.

Before responding, Layla squinted at the list, looking for her name. Sure enough, it was at the top of the list—she had achieved a perfect score. Once again, she was the top student in the department.
“Why, thank you,” she said, smiling courteously, as she turned back towards Count Lemon. She tried to effect the most lofty, aristocratic demeanor that she could, in imitation of her husband. “It’s so kind of you to congratulate me first.” Her voice was shaking slightly, but she managed to maintain her composure.
Up until the previous semester, she had been trying not to make enemies with him. Perhaps she would have still been trying now, if, a few weeks back, he hadn’t made the mistake of insulting Felix.
“You’re always so generous to your fellow students, Count Lemon. I really appreciate how you keep allowing me to have the glory of first place every semester. Thanks to you, I think I’m going to have a wonderful summer break.”
With each elegantly sarcastic word she spoke, his face grew a little redder. “I hope you have a nice break as well,” she added, nodding to him in mock
respect.
She turned away from the wall with a bounce in her step. During the past year, she had gradually adjusted to college life as both the only female student in the biology department, and, naturally, as the only duchess in the entire university.
When she reached her bicycle in front of the building, she climbed onto it and started pedaling with more strength than usual. Her leather bag, its strap slung across her shoulders, shook against her back as she rode.
It wasn’t long before rumors about the bicycle-riding duchess reached all the way to Arvis. Elise and Katharina were shocked to hear about it, but Matthias didn’t bat an eye. After all, he was the one who had presented her

with a new bicycle, after she told him how much she disliked being driven to and from campus by a chauffeur.
She rode the shiny, chrome bicycle off campus, and proceeded towards the sweet-scented park. She would have gotten back to the mansion faster by using the boulevard downtown, but she didn’t mind going a little out of her way if it meant that she could pass through the park, with its beautiful forest path that reminded her so much of Arvis. Suddenly, she got excited as she remembered that she would be spending the summer in Arvis.
It had been her decision to stay at Arvis for her first summer break as a student. Matthias had suggested that they travel somewhere together as a late honeymoon, but she didn’t like the idea of Felix staying at Arvis without her. Most of all, however, she missed Arvis and its beautiful forest. Despite all the pain and sadness she had experienced there, the precious memories she had from growing up there meant that it would always feel like home to her.
When the mansion’s gates came into view up ahead, she started pedaling faster. For the first time in quite a while, Matthias had the day off work today. She couldn’t wait to brag about her grade to him. She knew how childish this desire was, but she wasn’t ashamed. She knew he wouldn’t mind. He was Matthias, after all.
As she reached the front of the mansion, a middle-aged maid curtsied to her and said, “Hello, Your Grace. His Grace is in the garden. The young master is with him.”
Layla’s eyes widened, first with surprise, then with delight. “Did you say that Felix is with him?”
Perhaps Matthias had been joking when he suggested that they go off traveling without Felix.

She cut straight across the mansion’s main hall, towards the back door that let out into the garden. Her braided hair bounced with her quick steps. As she walked, she pictured the heartwarming scene she was about to encounter.
She reached the garden and stepped onto the walking path. There was a gentle breeze carrying a strong, rosy scent.
Finally, she reached the rose-covered pergola. Underneath it were her husband and their son, who looked like a little version of his father. At the sight, she burst into joyful laughter.




