Side Story: Bird of Paradise (2).





Matthias slowly turned his head in the direction of the laughter. His assistant, who was standing next to him, reporting on some business
matters, also turned to look at Layla.
Layla’s eyes shifted from the two men to the post on the opposite side of the pergola, where Felix von Herhardt was standing with his hands behind his back, solemnly watching the two men. It was almost as if he understood what they were discussing. Matthias followed Layla’s gaze, and let out a chuckle when he saw Felix.
Including the assistant, there were a few servants present. Quickly grasping the situation, they quietly retreated, leaving the three-person family to themselves under the pergola.
“Look at that,” Layla said, as she strode towards the father and son. “I guess you can’t stand to be without Felix either, huh?”

Felix turned and, seeing his mother smiling at him with her arms spread wide, he toddled excitedly over to her and into her arms.
Savoring the sight of the mother and son together, Matthias set the documents he was holding down on the table next to him. He decided not to tell Layla that it had been the nanny’s decision to bring Felix out to the garden. He didn’t want to spoil the beautiful look of joyful appreciation in her eyes.
“I have something to tell you,” she said, as she carried Felix over to the table and sat down with him across from Matthias. Her green eyes were twinkling with excitement. “Try to guess what it is,” she added in a quiet, mysterious tone.
Rather than revealing that he already knew what she was going to say, Matthias played along, furrowing his brow.
“Hmm…”
Felix, who had been giggling and tugging on his mother’s braided hair, noticed the serious look on his father’s face, and immediately furrowed his brow in imitation.
“I give up. Why don’t you tell me, Layla?” Matthias said, leaning forward to show his interest in what she had to say.
She smiled bashfully, and soon her cheeks were bright red.
Layla, he mouthed, as a smile spread across his lips. Layla. My Layla.







The train from the capital city arrived at Karlsbar Station. A group of Arvis employees, led by the butler, Hessen, hurried to line up in front of the special

class car. Many passersby on the platform, noticing the crowd of servants, looked expectantly at the train’s door. Soon, they saw the face of the young man they were expecting: Duke Herhardt, Arvis’s lord. As excited as they were to see him, they were even more excited to see the new duchess and their son.
“Hello, Your Grace,” Hessen said, bowing to his master with utmost courtesy, as always. The servants behind him bowed as well.
Layla still wasn’t used to this treatment. She squeezed Matthias’s arm nervously. The platform in front of the special-class car was already crowded with people trying to catch a glimpse of the young Herhardt family.
While Layla tried to catch her breath, Matthias nodded his head in response to the butler’s greeting. He seemed very relaxed, as if he hadn’t even noticed the crowd of gawking spectators. In other words, he was behaving like the perfect gentleman that the people of Karlsbar had come to expect.
While the onlookers exchanged expectant glances, Matthias stepped forward. His disregard for the onlookers made his elegant movements even more striking as he escorted his wife through the crowd. Everyone reflexively stepped back to clear a way for them to pass through.
Looking utterly dignified, Duke Herhardt’s demeanor was worlds apart from the flawed man he was portrayed as in the rumors about his scandalous marriage. The same was true of the duchess.
Wearing a turquoise sundress, Layla von Herhardt looked like any other lady her age. Despite her new status as Duchess Herhardt, she was clearly the same young lady who had grown up by Arvis’s forest, raised by the gardener.
The crowd’s attention then shifted to the nanny following behind the couple, and specifically to the future duke she was carrying in her arms. The little boy’s blue eyes shone with delight as he took in the sights around him. There had been a scandalous rumor going around that the new duchess was a

seductress who had fooled the duke into taking in a child from a different father. Now that everyone saw the incredible resemblance between duke and heir, however, this rumor could be decisively put to rest.
Before leaving the platform, Felix von Herhardt waved eagerly at the spectators. This innocent display was met with adoring laughter from the crowd.
“Summer has arrived in Karlsbar at last,” one of the onlookers said. “You’re right,” someone else replied.
For the people of Karlsbar, the return to Arvis of the House of Herhardt’s masterpiece marked the beginning of summer each year in that northern city.







After gazing out the car window at the scenery passing by for a while, Layla suddenly turned towards Matthias and asked, “Remember the gift you promised me?”
Matthias, who had already been gazing at his wife, cocked his head slightly in response.
“You said that we could go on a picnic this weekend. All three of us,” she reminded him. The look on her face grew more serious as he continued to remain silent.
Meanwhile, he was just enjoying the beauty of her emerald-colored eyes, even though they looked a little darker now than usual.
While boasting of her grades to him, she had excitedly added that she had thought of a gift she wanted from him. She wanted to go on a picnic to Arvis’s

forest, just the three of them.
“This time I’ll really show you the field with all the flowers, ’’she had cautiously added. “There are so many pretty flowers that bloom in the summer at Arvis!”
It hadn’t taken long for him to grasp her meaning. She smiled, and he nodded knowingly.
Layla, he thought, do you realize that you saved my life when you told me that the affection you showed me before we first parted wasn’t a lie?
“Don’t feign ignorance,” she said, narrowing her eyes at him as she scrutinized the expression on his face. “You’re messing with me, aren’t you?” she asked, in the same stern tone she used when scolding Felix.
Unlike Felix, however, Matthias laughed in response, which made her feel silly.
“I remember, Layla,” he finally said.
His unexpectedly gentle tone made her flinch.
He stopped smiling and, gazing at her with a calm, contemplative expression, added, “I remember everything about you.
She often felt overwhelmed with emotion when he said profound things like this to her in such a dry, casual tone.
“You almost had me there,” she said, speaking more forcefully than before. “But I know you’re just teasing me.”
“Am I?” he slyly replied, still staring straight into her eyes.
She stared right back at him resolutely, until finally she couldn’t stand it and had to look away. Moments like this had been occurring more frequently lately— moments that felt both comfortable and uncomfortable at once. This perplexed her, but it didn’t upset her.
While she mulled over this strange feeling, the car turned onto Platanus Road. They were almost to the gates of Arvis. The smile returned to her face as

she looked out the window. “Look, Felix! Isn’t it pretty?”
Sitting on his mother’s lap, Felix looked with amazement at the things she was pointing to.
“That’s a plane tree,” she said. “And that bird? It’s a thrush. And those little white flowers? They’re called baby’s breath.”
Felix babbled along with his mother, trying his best to repeat the terms she was teaching him. Layla gently corrected his pronunciation, laughing, and he laughed along with her. Their mutual laughter made Arvis’s summer even more beautiful.
As Matthias listened to this beautiful sound, his eyes settled on the spot on the road where, several summers earlier, his life had been turned upside down when Layla fell from her bicycle. Still vivid in his memory were the sounds of the overturned bicycle’s wheels spinning in the air, the leaves rustling in the breeze, and most of all, the pounding of his heart.
At the time, he had assumed that his passion for Layla was just a transitory thing that would soon pass. Instead, it had come to dominate his entire life. He now accepted this passion as an indelible part of himself, and he loved the beautiful days that he lived under its sway.
Leaving Platanus Road behind, the car passed through the shining gates of Arvis and proceeded towards the mansion. Elise and Katharina were standing in front of the mansion’s front entrance, ready to greet the family. After being carried out of the car by his nanny, Felix was immediately passed into his grandmother Elise’s arms.
“Welcome home, Matthias,” Katharina said, smiling brightly at her grandson. Her face retained every bit of benevolence as she turned towards the new duchess. “And welcome home, Layla.”

It was a starkly different greeting from the cold one she had given Layla the previous summer. Elise von Herhardt, who had never previously tried to hide her dissatisfaction with her daughter-in-law, was also showing nothing but warmth towards her now.
Before entering the mansion, Layla stared for a moment at the foot of the stairs. It was the spot where years earlier, as a young orphan girl, she had looked with both fear and curiosity at the young duke.
Who would have imagined that this young girl would have become the next duchess? If someone had predicted this to her back then, she would have thought that they were delusional.
In fact, she could still barely believe it was true. To think that, after all that time of only being able to be with Matthias in the shadows, she was now living with him in the bright, open light of day, as his wife, Duchess Layla von Herhardt! Sometimes, it all felt like a dream. In such moments, it was usually Matthias who pulled her back to reality.
“Layla?”
She slowly turned her head at the sound of her husband’s voice. She took a deep breath, and as she exhaled, her surroundings became clear to her again.
The couple walked side by side into the mansion’s main hall. The lavish marble staircase and the dazzling light of the chandelier were as intimidating as ever, but Layla paid them little mind as she walked. Rather than letting those things distract her, she was making a list in her mind of all the things she wanted to do that summer.
When the three of us go on our picnic, we should take a walk along the path where all the wild rose bushes grow. The field at the end of that path is as beautiful as a painting, with its birch trees and violets. Oh, and when the sun starts to set, we can

watch the flocks of birds flying above the Schulter River. But for that, the most beautiful view is up in the big tree next to the river. Can Matthias handle that?
With a look of solemn concern on her face, she whispered, “Matty?” He looked at her.
“Do you know how to climb trees?” she asked, making sure to stay quiet enough that she wouldn’t be heard by Elise and Katharina, who were walking in front of them.
He furrowed his brow slightly.
“If you don’t know how, I can teach you,” she added, still looking utterly serious. “Would you like that?”
He stared blankly at her for a moment, hardly believing his ears.
“I won’t force you to do it if you don’t want to, of course,” she hastened to add. “I’ll respect your wishes.” She could sense that she was digging herself into a deeper and deeper hole with each thing she said, but there was no backing out now.
Meanwhile, the couple arrived at the hallway that connected the central staircase to the drawing room.
Suddenly, he bowed his head reverently and said, “I will follow whatever orders you give, Your Majesty.”
A look of embarrassment spread across her face as she realized what he was doing.
Don’t do that! she mouthed.
Paying no attention to her plea, he added, “My queen.”
He was whispering so quietly that only Layla could hear him, but that didn’t stop her from feeling embarrassed.
She glowered at him, but he just smiled back.

What a strange guy he is, she thought, reconfirming this point to herself, before finally starting to laugh.
Just then, Elise and Katharina looked back at her. She could tell at once why they were looking only at her. As soon as they had started turning, Matthias had stopped smiling and assumed a stately, dignified posture as he looked at her, as if he had no idea why his wife was laughing.
Rather than scolding her daughter-in-law, Elise turned to the toddler in her arms and said, “Felix, your mother seems to be in quite a good mood today.” With that, she turned back around.
Layla felt like a fool laughing alone, but she knew that trying to explain the situation would only make things worse.
Once his mother and grandmother were facing away from them again, Matthias flashed another calm smile at his scowling wife. Suppressing the urge to shout the truth to everyone, she just squeezed the arm of her irritating husband.
She could tell it was going to be a long summer.




