Side Story: Bird of Paradise (3).





Layla rode her bicycle down the forest path alone, surrounded by birdsong. When she reached its end, she saw the cottage.
She took off her hat and stood at the edge of the front yard, slowly taking in the sight. Even though no one had lived there for quite some time, the cottage looked just the same as when it had been her home.
Katharina and Elise had wanted to have the unused cottage demolished and replaced with a garden where they could have outdoor tea parties, but Matthias had firmly opposed this.
After hearing about this from Matthias’s assistant, Layla had gone to Matthias and asked, “What are you planning to do with Uncle Bill’s cottage?”
“Why are you asking me?” he asked, looking at her from across the desk in his study. To her surprise, he then said, “It’s up to you. ”As his gaze returned to

the stack of documents on his desk, he said in an even calmer voice, “Whatever you want to do with it, that’s what will be done. ”
Taken aback, she paused for a moment, then replied, “So you’re saying that Uncle Bill’s cottage is mine?”
In response, he briefly glanced up at her and nodded, before resuming his work.
As he turned page after page, she slowly processed his response, before finally understanding the meaning of his simple nod.
She wondered how he felt about the situation, but she didn’t ask. He seemed too engrossed in his work anyway. When she thought back on that day, the sound of him flipping through the documents echoed in her mind.
She checked her watch, then started riding across the yard towards the cottage. She still had plenty of time before her teatime with Dowager Herhardt.
She got down from her bicycle and leaned it against the porch, then entered the well- maintained cottage and slowly surveyed the interior. Most of the items that she and Uncle Bill had left behind had been gathered up and transported to the mansion in Ratse. All that remained were several larger pieces of furniture with dust covers on them. It was a lonely sight.
She decided to head out onto the porch. The two different-sized chairs had not been moved from their usual spots. She sat in her chair and looked over at Uncle Bill’s large, wooden chair. She could almost see him slouching there, smoking a cigarette. Then she thought she could hear his gruff but kind voice, calling her name and laughing.
She and Uncle Bill had sat next to each other on this porch countless times, chatting together as they enjoyed the fresh air. She decided to strike up a conversation. “Uncle Bill, I’m going to college, just like you hoped I would. All

the other students are male, but your Layla is number one in the class. Aren’t you proud?”
Chatting like this felt like the most natural thing in the world.
“Felix is doing well. He’s growing so fast! It feels like just yesterday that he took his first steps, wearing the shoes you got him. But now he’s running and talking! You must know that already, though. I’d like to have one more child who could wear those shoes, but my husband feels otherwise.”
She continued chatting like this for a long while, just like she had done so many times before. She talked about college life, about Felix, and about Matthias. Over time, her voice started to shake, but her smile grew more peaceful.
Finally, she opened her eyes and stood up. “I’ll be back another time.”
The afternoon sunlight cast a long shadow on Uncle Bill’s well-worn chair. “I love you, Dad,” she said, before slowly stepping down from the porch.
As she put on her hat and got back on her bicycle, she felt a burst of energy. She started pedaling at a brisk pace. As she soared back up the forest path, the skirt of her white dress flapped in the breeze.
She had not yet decided what to do with the cottage, but she was sure of one thing: it needed to become a place that would bring Uncle Bill joy.
With a look of determination on her face, she raised her eyes towards the sky. She could almost hear him laughing heartily and saying, My little Layla has become an outstanding adult.







The car carrying the Herhardt family emerged from the forest road and came to a stop by the Schulter River. The servants who had come in advance to prepare everything stood in a line to greet their master.
The chauffeur hurried to open the back passenger door. The first family member to exit the car was Matthias. Wearing a cream-colored linen suit, he looked as dignified as ever, like the flawless Duke Herhardt that everyone knew.
While the servants quietly admired their master’s attire, a small foot wearing a strappy shoe emerged from the car. Out of habit, the chauffeur started approaching, then he abruptly stopped himself. Duke Herhardt didn’t allow anyone but himself to escort his wife. All the employees of Arvis were aware of this rule.
Who would have thought that little Layla, the gardener’s adopted daughter, would become the next duchess? Most of the servants present still thought of her as Arvis’s child. They looked at the young couple with amazement.
Layla took the duke’s outstretched hand and stepped out of the car. The ample skirt of her dress swayed with the gentle movement. Her green eyes were as warm as the summer sun as she took in the scenery. “It’s a beautiful day!”
The duke’s austere face softened in response to his wife’s cheerful exclamation, and his whole bearing seemed to shift subtly, becoming less cold and stiff.

Some of the Arvis employees had already wondered if something had changed in the duke’s personality. They now realized that this change was entirely due to his love for his wife, Layla von Herhardt. Far from being a sign of weakness, this only made him seem more sturdy and secure in the life he was leading with her.
The duke and duchess started walking side by side, and the servants followed behind them. Even with so many people traveling along it, the forest path was quiet and serene. Listening to the clear sound of the birds and the breeze, Layla looked over at Matthias. He was looking straight ahead as they sauntered down the path.
With bated breath, she watched his face as light and shadow alternated upon it. Soon, she realized that although he was facing forward, his eyes were looking straight at her. Her lips quivered for a moment, then she lowered her eyes, saying nothing. He had kept his promise. The outing was a little different from the one she had envisioned, but she didn’t feel the need to mention this. It was just fine this way.
She looked into his eyes again and smiled, and then looked over her shoulder and gave an identical smile to their son, who was being pushed in a carriage by the nanny. No longer fussy about riding in the carriage like he once had been, Felix was cheerfully taking in the sights around him. Resisting the urge to run towards him and give him a hug, Layla turned to face ahead again.
There was a distinctly different order to things in Arvis, compared to the Ratse mansion. The former duchesses were allowing the couple to live their lives however they wanted in Ratse. When they stayed at Arvis, however, things were more strict. By now, Layla had grown accustomed to this. She was also well aware of Dowager Herhardt’s requirement that the duke’s successor should learn to live in both environments.

When they arrived at the picnic site, Layla gasped in amazement. It was a quiet spot by a brook where she used to take breaks from picking raspberries. Now, however, it looked completely different. There was a large picnic mat spread across the ground. Atop it was an array of items that seemed completely out of place in the forest: a small stove with a tea kettle, ornate dishes and utensils, cushions and blankets, and a vase full of fresh flowers. Even though she herself had chosen the location for the picnic, she couldn’t help but feel like she was visiting a place she had never been to before.
“It’s like you took an entire room from the mansion and transported it here intact,” she said.
Matthias chuckled in response. The gentle expression on his face made him look even more like Felix. Perhaps that was why she kept feeling her heart flutter when she looked at him.
“Thank you,” she said. It seemed like it was going to be more burdensome of a picnic than she had expected, but she wasn’t going to let that spoil their time together. “Thank you for keeping your promise.
