Side Story: Bird of Paradise (17).





The news of Duchess Herhardt’s pregnancy spread quickly throughout the high society of Karlsbar and Ratse. The duke and duchess were a young, healthy couple, so there was nothing particularly remarkable about them having a second child. What was causing a stir was the fact that the duchess
was continuing to attend college while pregnant.
“At the very least, we can expect the child to be exceptionally intelligent,” Elise von Herhardt remarked. This was all that she said on the matter.
Katharina von Herhardt’s feelings were more or less the same. Both women knew that there was no point in putting up meaningless opposition, since Matthias was not going to reverse his decision no matter what they said. While they had worried that his marriage to Layla would ruin his reputation, they actually respected the way he was sticking to his guns now.

“If you’ve already decided to do that, you might as well give it your all,” Elise had unexpectedly said a few days earlier while talking to Layla on the phone. “As long as you’re living as a Herhardt, you mustn’t let anyone ignore you. Got it ?”
Elise was speaking with her usual quiet, unemotional voice, but by now Layla had learned to detect the subtle variations in her tone. When she was speaking from her heart, she spoke even more slowly and gently than usual.
“Of course!” Layla replied, more enthusiastically than she had intended to. “I’ll try my absolute best, I promise.”
Elise clicked her tongue and sighed at Layla’s unladylike tone, but she didn’t go as far as to scold her.
Layla truly intended to try her best, so that she could keep her promise. Of course, she knew that some things couldn’t be overcome by sheer force of will, such as morning sickness. Her morning sickness had subsided for a while. When it started up again, she closed her book and lay down on the sofa. After what felt like only a brief nap, she opened her eyes to discover that the sun had set, and Matthias was by her side. When she realized that she was lying with her head on his lap, a bright smile spread across her pale face.
As soon as their eyes met, he asked, “Did you eat?” Ever since her morning sickness had begun, this question had become his way of greeting her.
“Yes, I ate a little today,” she replied.
“Peaches again?” he asked, glancing at the basket of peaches on the table.
Throughout her pregnancy, their bedroom had been suffused with the sweet scent of peaches.
Layla had delivered the news of her pregnancy to Matthias the night of their late dinner together. As she told him, her eyes shone with joyous anticipation, as though she were giving him a surprise gift. He was very careful to react in such a way that she wouldn’t realize he already knew.

Her behavior that morning had given him a hunch that there was something she was holding back from telling him. His hunch was confirmed that evening, when he talked on the phone with Dr. Peller, the family doctor.
“Can you make a house call? I think my wife is pregnant.” Matthias said.
The doctor remained silent for quite a while, before replying, ‘Your Grace, you already knew?” He sounded quite confused. “Your wife asked me to keep it a secret, because she wanted to tell you herself…”
“Yes, I figured,” Matthias replied, remaining calm as he spoke to the flustered doctor. “That’s fine. I’ll pretend that I don’t know about it.”
It wasn’t hard to guess what Layla was hoping for. She wanted to give him a wonderful surprise, and he wanted to let her think that she had.
After exchanging a few formalities with the doctor, Matthias calmly got to the heart of the matter “When she was pregnant with our first child, she had some health issues” As he recalled helplessly watching as Layla feverishly hovered on the brink of death, the memories felt more traumatic than his memories of the war itself. “This time around, I hope she has no such issues ~ he added in a flat lone- ~Of course, I’m sure that with you looking after her, Dr. Peller, there is no cause for concern.”
“I will do everything I can to help your wife have a healthy pregnancy and delivery,’” the doctor replied.
With that, their phone conversation concluded.
From then on, without even needing to be asked. Dr. Peller made frequent house calls to check up on the duchess’s health. He reported that, other than her perfectly natural morning sickness, she was completely healthy, as was the fetus inside her.
Everything was going well, at least so far.

The night when Matthias returned to the mansion, ready to act surprised at the news of Layla’s pregnancy, he found it somewhat awkward.
Layla was evasive throughout dinner, repeatedly changing the subject. As time wore on, the anticipation in her eyes grew as she looked at him.
As he waited for her to deliver the news, he remained uncertain how he should react. He started to ponder over his true feelings on the matter. Was he happy about it? That was an easy question. Of course he was happy about it. His second child was developing in the womb of the woman he loved. This was Layla’s ardent wish. However, he suspected that she was expecting him to have stronger feelings about the situation than he did. To him, it was a fairly straightforward matter. They had put forth the effort to conceive a child, and their efforts had been a success. It was a natural, predictable outcome.
Only after they were completely done with dinner did she finally reveal the long-awaited news. He reacted with awkward silence, still uncertain how he should respond. Luckily, this turned out to be the perfect response, as she assumed he was simply overwhelmed with joy.
She guided him towards the proper response by spreading open her arms and saying, “Would you give me a hug? I’d like that” Her beautiful green eyes shone with cautious anticipation.
He immediately pulled his lovely wife into his embrace and hugged her tightly.
A little later that night, while she was still in his arms, she requested a gift from him. To his surprise, all she wanted was a peach.
“I know it doesn’t sound like much, but it’s exactly what I want” she said, staring straight into his eyes. “I just want a crisp, sweet peach, with no bruises.”
He found it hard to believe that this was all she wanted, but he gladly complied with her request, giving her not just one peach but a whole basket of

them. She accepted the basket and held it to her chest with a look of transcendent delight on her face. She then set down the basket and carefully inspected the peaches, looking for the prettiest one of the bunch.
Observing the serious look on her face, he laughed and said, “They’re all yours, Layla.”
“I know, but still…” Still looking very serious, she held out two peaches towards him, one in each hand, and asked, “Which one of these would you say is prettier?”
To him, they looked more or less identical, but he could tell that the question was very important to her.
“You pick one,” she said. “I want to eat the prettiest one first.”
Not wanting to disappoint her, he carefully scrutinized the identical- looking peaches, then selected the one in her left hand, which seemed ever so slightly brighter-colored and shinier.
She then proceeded to stare intently for a long while at the peach he had designated as the prettiest. Seeing what seemed to be complex emotions in her eyes, he wondered what she was thinking, but he didn’t ask. Instead he just stood quietly by her side, waiting for her eyes to turn back towards him.
Fortunately, it wasn’t long before the smile returned to her face. Then she carefully, ceremoniously started eating the peach.
As he watched her nibble at the fruit, chewing each crispy, fragrant bite with care, he found himself becoming enraptured by the sight. Whenever he thought back to that night, this was the memory that popped into his mind first. And so, whenever he thought about the baby growing inside her belly, he could feel the sweet scent of peach juice tickling his nose, as was happening right now.




“Yes, I had some peaches, but I ate other food too,” Layla replied in a slow, drowsy voice.
Slowly caressing her hair, Matthias asked, “What sort of other food?” “I had some eggs and a little chicken. And some bread, too”
“Hmm, I don’t think those aren’t the best things to eat when you’ve got an upset stomach,” he remarked, furrowing his brow as he looked down at her.
“Well, I requested them specifically, so don’t get upset with anyone else,” she replied, looking nervously up at him. “I did some research on fetal development, and I learned that morning sickness tends to subside once the placenta is fully formed. Eggs and meat are supposed to be especially helpful to that end. And they’re good for the fetus as well "
“Fine, but isn’t it unpleasant eating food that makes you sick?”
“It is, but I’d prefer feeling very sick for a short time over feeling a little sick for a long time. After all, I have exams coming up.”
“Sounds like a thoroughly scientific conclusion.”
“Yes, it’s quite logical, don’t you think?” Giggling, she sat up. Before long, however, her face went pale. It looked like she was going to be sick again.
“You should lie down in bed,” he said, as she put her head back on his lap. “No, I prefer the sofa.” she stubbornly replied, shaking her head. “My
nausea gets worse when I lie in bed.”
“What’s the difference between the bed and the sofa?” he asked. “Well, I’m not sure exactly… It’s just a feeling I’ve gotten,” she replied.
He chuckled at the contrast between the intelligent look in her eyes and the nonsensical things coming out of her mouth.

“Listen, I understand that what I’m saying might sound a little irrational,” she said, frowning as she acknowledged the disparity between her thoroughly analytic decision to eat eggs and meat despite the nausea they caused, and her intuitive, emotional decision to avoid lying in bed during the day. “But I can’t help it. I bet you couldn’t stay perfectly rational either if you had morning sickness.” It seemed like she had more to say, but she went silent and shut her eyes.
Gently stroking her pale, sweat-beaded forehead, he let out a silent sigh.
After a while, she opened her eyes again and asked, “Matty, would you read my book to me?”
He furrowed his brow once again. “Your book?”
“Yes, it’s over there on the table,” she replied, pointing at a thick book on paleontology, next to the peach basket. ”I haven’t gotten through all the parts that will be on the exam yet. I’d like you to read it to me.”
Snickering and shaking his head, he opened the book, which was sticky with her peach-juice fingerprints.
The peach-scented air was soon filled with the sound of his voice explaining the evolution of ancient life forms.
With her eyes closed, she hung on his every word. Even though his reading was interrupted now and then by the sound of her gulping to keep from retching, it was overall a very pleasant night.







As winter break approached, Layla developed a reputation in her classes comparable to the reputation her husband held in the business world. Some of her classmates started calling her the ruthless duchess. As if attending classes while pregnant weren’t enough, she had achieved top-student status once again. Her rival students now viewed her less with contempt than with fear.
However, as she stood with her arms around her bulbous belly, looking at the list of names and grades, she didn’t look frightening at all. On the contrary, she looked positively friendly and welcoming. Even as some of her rivals congratulated her with obvious sarcasm, she smiled back at them and thanked them sincerely.
“You’re certainly far from the average girl,” Elise von Herhardt replied, after Layla had called and told her all about the semester. “Way to go, Layla.” Elise’s voice softened noticeably through the course of the conversation.
Layla thought back on that short phone call many times. Each time she did, she felt a wave of joy, and she could feel the fetus kicking, as if it was feeling joyous as well.
“Matty!” she shouted, after feeling the fetus kick once again. ‘She’s kicking!
Come quick!”
She hoped that he would hurry, but he walked into the room at his usual leisurely pace.
As he sat on the edge of the bed, she grabbed his hand and placed it on her belly. “She’s kicking. You can feel it, right?”

He patiently sat and felt her belly for a long time, because he could tell that she wanted him to. It wasn’t the first time the fetus had kicked, so it wasn’t a particularly remarkable occasion, but he greatly enjoyed seeing how excited she got about it.
She continued holding his hand firmly against her belly until the kicking finally stopped. Even after his hand was free, he continued to sit on the edge of the bed, gazing at his beautiful wife. The glimmering light from the fireplace made the curve of her round belly even more distinct.
He went and got a warm washcloth. When he touched it to her chest, she flinched and said, “I can do it.” However, he ignored her and proceeded to gently wipe down her body.
Once she realized that further resistance was futile, she submitted to his soft touch. Even though the washcloth felt pleasantly warm against her skin, her heart was beating at a steadily increasing pace.
By the time he finished, her cheeks were flushed bright red. He continued to gaze at her body. The sight of her big, round belly gave him a somewhat different feeling this time than it had the first time she was pregnant. This time, the feeling was one of languid satisfaction.
He carefully dressed her in her nightgown, and then lay down on the bed with her in his arms. Instead of struggling like in the past, she calmly buried her face in his neck. By now it was a regular occurrence for them to fall asleep holding each other like this, with their legs gently intertwined.
“A few weeks from now, I’m really going to look like a penguin.” she whispered in a worried tone. “I don’t know if you’ll still find me pretty.” But I hope you’ll call me pretty anyway, she wanted to add. Feeling too embarrassed to say this, she hesitated for a moment.

To her surprise, however, he seemed to have guessed what she was thinking. “I’ll still tell you you’re pretty,” he quietly assured her. “I’ll tell you that every day, Layla.”




