Side Story: Bird of Paradise (18).





The newest member of the Herhardt family arrived with the spring, on a warm, sunny afternoon when the trees were packed with fresh flower
buds.
A hospital attendant approached with swift, soft steps. “It was a safe, easy delivery. They’re almost ready for you. Your Grace.”
Matthias set down the tea cup he was holding, and turned towards the door where the attendant was standing. The attendant offered some words of congratulation, then left. In his place, Dr. Peller then appeared, with his medical team following behind him. After reporting on the mother and baby’s condition and offering their congratulations, this group left as well.
The VIP suite’s waiting room fell silent again, now that only Herhardts remained there. Matthias gazed out the window at the bulging flower buds on the tree branches, while his mother and grandmother gazed at him. Felix, full

of excitement at the prospect of meeting his new sibling, had run around all morning until he tired himself out. Now he was sound asleep.
“Congratulations, Matthias. You’re now the father of two children,” Katharina said, her soft voice filling the room along with the bright, spring sunlight.
Only then did Matthias turn to look at his mother and grandmother, who were sitting across the table from him.
He had come straight from work to the hospital. His clothing and hair were immaculate, and he bore the facial expression and posture of a perfect gentleman, as usual. To an onlooker, it would have been impossible to believe that he was a man who had just heard that the love of his life was in labor. Before he arrived, his mother and grandmother had worried that the stress of the moment might have caused him to lose some of the dignified appearance expected of him as a duke. To the contrary, it was now the utter flawlessness of his appearance that was astounding them.
“Thank you, Grandmother, and Mother,” he replied, calmly looking into each woman’s eyes. The gentle smile on his lips also looked the same as always.
They exchanged a few more pleasantries, and soon the attendant returned and said that the birthing room had been tidied up and was ready for visitors.
“You go see Layla and the baby first, Matthias. We’ll stay here and wait with Felix. Once he’s awake, we’ll come and say hello,” Elise said, stroking the hair of her sleeping grandson as she held him in her arms. She had no idea what was going on in Matthias’s head, but she knew that Layla would want to see her husband before anyone else.
Matthias nodded and rose from his seat. He seemed strangely cold and aloof as he straightened his clothes and left the waiting room.

“I know that young man is my flesh and blood, but I must say, I don’t understand him at all,“Elise said, looking with narrowed eyes at Katharina. “After he went to all that trouble arranging that special gift for Layla, why is he acting so cold today?”
At the beginning of the previous month, Matthias had told Elise and Katharina that he intended to have Bill Remmer’s grave transported from Lovita to Berg. He even planned to enlist the aid of the Lovita royal family to ensure that it would be done as quickly as possible.
