Looking at her smile, he knew he wasn’t dreaming. Back when she first ran away to Lovita and he was wasting away with longing for her, he had tried and tried to picture her sweet smile in his head, but his imagination had never managed to produce anything half as beautiful as the smile he now saw before him.
He crossed the threshold and, walking to her bedside, hugged her tightly.
She lifted her quivering arms and wrapped them around his back.
Their embrace lasted until a nurse approached, holding the baby wrapped in swaddling clothes.
“Isn’t she pretty?” Layla whispered with a shaky voice as she took the baby into her arms and smiled brightly with tears in her eyes. “It’s our daughter, Leah.”

The baby stirred in her sleep, as though she liked hearing her mother say her name.
“Leah…” As Matthias whispered the name he had given their daughter, his face softened into a gentle smile, and he slowly caressed her hair.
Their little princess, Leah von Herhardt, had hair just like her mother’s, blonde and unbelievably soft.







There was a rose bush growing next to the gardener’s grave. Layla planted it herself the summer after the grave was relocated. After over a year growing there, the bush had grown bigger and fuller, and its blossoms were even more beautiful. They were the lovely, pale-pink roses that he cherished more than any other variety.
“Mommy! It’s Grandpa!” Felix shouted confidently as he stood in front of the gravestone.
Layla smiled at him and nodded. “Wow. you remembered! You’re so smart, Felix!”
“And those are roses!” he said, encouraged by bis mother’s praise. He then proceeded to look around and name everything he laid eyes on-the sky, the trees, the birds, the clouds. After saying each word loudly to his mother, he then turned to his little sister, who was toddling around next to him, and quietly repeated each term to her. Leah could only babble in response, but Felix didn’t give up on instructing her.

While the two siblings were chattering away like a pair of birds, Layla caught Uncle Bill up about everything that had happened since she last talked to him. She told him about college life. She told him about the various cute, silly things the children had been up to. She told him how the Arvis staff were doing. And she told him about Matthias. She was now able to talk to Uncle Bill about her husband without guilt or anxiety.
Matthias had waited to tell her about the grave relocation until the whole project was complete. When he told her, she first reacted with shock, staring at him speechlessly. Then, once she processed the meaning of what he had told her, she broke down in tears. That day, she was finally relieved of the deep guilt and longing she had felt after leaving Uncle Bill in that far-off location.
“Felix! Leah!” she called.
The siblings rushed towards her at once, hand in hand. As she looked down at the four blue eyes gazing up at her, she couldn’t help but giggle.
People had started to call Leah von Herhardt “Blonde Matthias.”
Even Layla had started to use the nickname. It was an apt description- besides her blonde hair, which she had inherited from her mother, Leah resembled her father to a remarkable extent.
Recently, in a conversation with her mother-in-law, Layla had brought up her hope that if she and Matthias had a third child, that one might resemble her more than Matthias.
“Give up, Layla’” Elise replied, laughing. “Haven’t you noticed a pattern among the Herhardt family portraits?”
Layla sank into thought for a moment before realizing the meaning of Elise’s enigmatic question. All the members of the Herhardt family had remarkably similar facial features, just like Matthias and his children.

“The Herhardt bloodline is very potent. You should take it as a victory that Leah inherited your hair color,” Elise flatly said.
As Layla heard this, it occurred to her that the same was true of Matthias— he had inherited his mother’s hair color, but the rest of his features resembled his father’s.
