The indifferent expression on Elise’s face then gave way to a grin. “Sounds like you’re planning to continue your studies even after graduation, then?”
“Pardon?” Layla replied. “Oh… Yes, yes I am.’
“That’s fine, dear. Best not to let that big brain of yours got waste. I must say, I like the thought of Duchess Herhardt becoming a Ratse University professor.”
“A professor?” Layla asked, amazed at what she was hearing. She knew she wanted to continue her studies after graduation, but she hadn’t set any more concrete goals than that.
“Don’t be surprised, dear. Don’t you think you’d find it easier to become a professor than to become the queen of high society?” Elise asked, playfully raising one eyebrow.
In that moment, Layla realized that Matthias had inherited more than just his hair color from his mother. He had gotten his sense of humor from her as well.
“Study hard, dear,” Elise said, speaking in a quiet tone like her son often did. “As for the cottage, do whatever you like with it.”







“Daddy!” Felix excitedly shouted when he saw the black car parked in front of the riverside cabin.
Layla and Leah looked up from the flowers they had been examining on the side of the trail As soon as the chauffeur opened the rear passenger door, Matthias stepped out of the car. It seemed that he had not forgotten the promise he had made to take the children out boating.
Layla quickly took out her handkerchief and wiped the dew from her fingers. Before she finished, Felix had already started running towards his father. Leah excitedly toddled behind him.
Layla took up the rear, watching the children to make sure they didn’t stumble and fall. The skirt of her turquoise sundress flapped gently in the breeze, as did the ends of her straw hat’s lace ribbon, which was tied under her chin. Her hair also bounced gently against the back of her neck as she walked.
The look in Matthias’s eyes deepened as he watched his wife and two children walking along the sunny riverside.
Suddenly he recalled something Layla had said to him recently while looking at Leah: “It makes no difference to me whether Leah looks more like you or like me. What I find amazing, though, is that she looks like you, even though you wanted her to took like me. Usually you always get what you want, but not this times”
He had wanted to reply that this actually was what he wanted, but instead he just laughed.

At first he had thought he wanted their daughter to look like Layla. After all, this was what Layla had said she wanted, and it was what most men who loved their wives would want.
However, at night, when he gazed at Layla’s face as she lay sleeping in his arms, he felt quite a different desire. He wanted there to be only one woman in the world who looked like Layla: Layla herself. He wanted to be the only man in the world who possessed a woman who looked like Layla. He wanted this now, in the future, and forevermore.
I guess she still drives me wild, he thought to himself, sighing, as he kissed his sleeping wife’s forehead, cheeks, and gently parted lips. As he softly rubbed the bridge of her nose and caressed her hair, he couldn’t help but laugh at himself. He felt ridiculous for failing to be a perfect husband no matter how hard he tried. His love for Layla was a little twisted, and for all he knew it would stay that way until the day he died. However, he didn’t mind this, as long as Layla remained his until the end.
He started walking towards his children as they rushed towards him. He scooped them both up into his arms. As soon as he did, Felix once again showed his innocent love by kissing him on the cheek. Matthias kissed him right back, and then kissed Leah, the new joy in his life.
Watching them with delight, Layla said, “Matty! You’re early!”
The smile on her face was the most radiantly beautiful thing he had ever seen.
Felix grew restless and started to struggle, so Matthias put him down. Still holding Leah, he walked up to Layla. They laughed warmly as they shared an intimate kiss.
He wanted Layla’s love to be like this—warm and peaceful enough for her to forget the challenges and loneliness of her childhood years, and for the

emotional wounds he had given her in the past to heal. He now knew that they both loved each other dearly, though perhaps in different ways. In fact, he didn’t care if he loved her more than she did him. He knew that the vast world of love contains areas of both light and shadow. If loving her more meant being in the shadows, he would gladly reside there, because his happiness was in her brilliant light.
He held out his hand, and she placed hers in it. The happy family of four walked along the edge of the glittering river, the father carrying the daughter, the mother holding the son’s hand.
As they passed under the shade of the large tree that was Layla’s favorite, Matthias slowly turned his head and looked back along the path they had just walked. His gaze shifted from the shadowy forest, to the glistening river, to the birds flying in the clear sky, before finally settling again on Layla’s face.
“Layla.”
As she turned her head upon hearing his voice, her smile grew even more radiant, and her eyes shimmered in the afternoon sunlight, mirroring the eternal summer of the forest. :) 

